  "query_preview": "..\nMonday: The Inland Northwest will remain between troughing off the \nPNW coast and a ridge over the central US for today. An enhanced \nthermal pressure gradient across the Cascades in the afternoon and \nevening will bring breezy to gusty conditions across central and \neastern Washington. Areas that will see the highest winds (25 to 30 \nmph) include the Okanogan Valley, Waterville Plateau, and Columbia \nBasin. With minimum relative humidity values 18 to 25 percent, \nelevated to critical fire weather conditions are expected in these \nareas. This will result in rapid fire spread for both new and \nexisting fires. Temperatures Monday will be in the mid to high 80s, \nright around normal for this time of year, resulting in only Minor \nHeatRisk. \n\nTuesday: On Tuesday, the low off the PNW coast will move inland, \nushering another dry cold front through the forecast area. This will \nbring widespread wind gusts 15 to 20 mph, and more localized higher \nwind gusts 25 to 35 mph in the usual areas: Cascade gaps, Waterville \nPlateau, Columbia Basin, and Okanogan Valley areas. These areas have \na 40 to 50 percent chance and above at seeing maximum wind gusts 35 \nmph or higher on Tuesday evening. With relative humidity values \nremaining low at 20-30 percent, this will bring elevated to isolated \ncritical fire weather conditions back to these areas. With relative \nhumidity values being slightly higher than Monday, fire weather \nheadlines are not anticipated. Temperatures on Tuesday remain near \nnormal. Along the Cascade crest, there is a 10-20 percent chance of \nlight precipitation. Elsewhere, expect continued dry conditions. \n\nWednesday through Friday: Once the cold front moves through, the \nregion will again be in between offshore troughing and a ridge over \nthe central US. The thermal pressure gradient will be weaker than on \nMonday and Tuesday, bringing breezy winds 10 to 20 mph to central \nand eastern Washington. However, the ridge will begin retrograding \nwestward and strengthening during the last two days of the work \nweek, resulting in increasing temperatures and a return to the 90s \non Friday. Friday looks to be the hottest day of the week, with \nModerate HeatRisk returning to parts of the region. This will also \nincrease the thermal pressure gradient, bringing slightly higher \nwind gusts near 25 mph throughout the basin, Waterville Plateau, and \nOkanogan Valley areas. \n\nNext weekend: Long-term guidance and clusters both favor another \ntrough moving across the region and ushering yet another dry cold \nfront through the area on Saturday. This will again increase winds \nacross the entire forecast area, and relative humidity values \nremaining low will result in another round of critical fire weather \nconditions. Areas that have the highest NBM probabilities (20 to 40 \npercent and above) for 40 mph wind gusts on Saturday are the upper \nColumbia Basin, Waterville Plateau, and Okanogan Valley areas.\nThroughout the week into next weekend, chances for precipitation \nremain at or near zero percent. /AS",
